{"id":"project_and_model_status","topic":"project_status","content":"Project: short-horizon NQ/MNQ futures research using 5-minute OHLC bars and hourly context; Qwen2.5-3B sequence classifier with BUY, SELL, HOLD classes. Dataset reported: 6,532 rows; HOLD 5,983, BUY 367, SELL 182. Chronological split reported: train 5,201 (HOLD 4,741, BUY 322, SELL 138), validation 1,307 (HOLD 1,222, BUY 41, SELL 44), with 24-bar purge. Latest reported adapter predicted HOLD for every validation row: confusion matrix actual rows/predicted columns [[0,0,41],[0,0,44],[0,0,1222]], accuracy about 93.5%. This matches the majority-class HOLD rate and demonstrates no directional skill on that validation set. Earlier adapter had BUY/SELL label-match precision 28.4% over 102 directional calls; this is label-match, not realized trade win rate."}
{"id":"targets_and_arithmetic","topic":"performance_targets","content":"Targets are evaluation criteria, not training labels or forecasts: 70% net win rate, average winner/average loser 2.2, expectancy 1.24R/trade, Sharpe 4.0-5.5+, max drawdown below 4%, profit factor 5.13+. Arithmetic before costs: 0.70*2.2R - 0.30*1R = 1.24R; gross profit factor = (0.70*2.2)/(0.30*1)=5.13. With round-trip cost c in R and fixed +2.2R/-1R outcomes, net expectancy is 0.70*(2.2-c)-0.30*(1+c). Actual outcomes vary due gaps, time exits, stop movement, slippage, fees, and execution. Never claim targets are met from classification accuracy."}
{"id":"evaluation_protocol","topic":"backtesting","content":"Use completed-bar information only; a signal at bar t close may enter no earlier than bar t+1 open. Freeze ATR at decision time. Model stop/target touches conservatively; when OHLC cannot establish barrier order, assume the adverse stop first. Charge entry and exit commission and slippage using the intended contract's economics. Track trades sequentially and prevent overlapping positions unless explicitly part of the strategy. Split chronologically, purge at least the 24-bar label horizon at boundaries, fit scalers/calibration only on training/development data, tune only inside development data, and preserve a final untouched chronological holdout for one evaluation. Register every strategy/threshold/model variant. Report trade count, coverage, net win rate, average winner/loser, expectancy in R, profit factor, daily-equity Sharpe with stated annualization, and marked-to-market drawdown. Use day-block bootstrap confidence intervals when enough independent days exist. Fewer than 200 out-of-sample trades means insufficient evidence, not a pass."}
{"id":"metric_distinctions","topic":"metrics","content":"Keep three quantities separate: (1) directional label-match precision compares model calls to generated BUY/SELL labels; (2) realized win rate is the fraction of executed trades with positive net P&L; (3) profitability includes net expectancy, profit factor, equity path, and costs. A majority HOLD classifier can have high accuracy and zero trades. A strategy's target distance is not its realized average winner. Sharpe requires a defined return series and frequency; do not annualize per-trade or 5-minute Sharpe with a naive square-root factor. Drawdown depends on equity sizing, open-position marking, trade order, leverage, and tail losses."}
{"id":"label_and_data_audit","topic":"data_quality","content":"Before more training, audit what HOLD means and verify generated labels against raw OHLC, ATR scale/timeframe, entry at next-bar open, 24-bar outcome horizon, and same-bar stop/target resolution. The reported 91.5-93.5% HOLD share is a warning to investigate, not proof of a bug. Check timestamps/time zone, missing bars, duplicate rows, contract roll treatment, completed hourly-bar alignment, and feature normalization leakage. Do not resample overlapping time-series observations with SMOTE-style interpolation without a justified temporal design. Compare per-class precision/recall and confusion matrices; class weights or threshold changes alone do not establish economic value."}
{"id":"hypothesis_A_session_momentum","topic":"strategy_hypothesis","content":"Research hypothesis A: RTH session momentum continuation. The research note proposes measuring the 09:30 ET open-to-15:00 ET return and comparing it with the prior 20 sessions' same-clock return variability; test a prespecified magnitude gate (including a separately registered sign-only variant), then enter at the next bar open in the indicated direction. Use fixed, frozen exits and force flat before the session close; no overnight position. This is motivated by published intraday-momentum research but the cited evidence is not proof of a 5-minute NQ edge. Requires reliable ET timestamps and enough multi-year sessions; evaluate chronologically and against clock-matched always-long and always-short nulls."}
{"id":"hypothesis_B_opening_range","topic":"strategy_hypothesis","content":"Research hypothesis B: 09:30-10:00 ET opening-range breakout. Freeze opening-range high/low after 10:00; on a completed-bar close beyond the range, enter next bar in breakout direction, at most one trade per day and before a prespecified cutoff. The volume-confirmed version requires per-bar volume and a time-of-day relative-volume baseline. If volume is unavailable, removing the volume filter creates a different, weaker fallback and cannot validate the original hypothesis. Treat cited strategy-level preprints as unrefereed hypothesis sources, not proof. Test both directions against clock-matched nulls."}
{"id":"hypothesis_C_volatility_pullback","topic":"strategy_hypothesis","content":"Research hypothesis C (exploratory): volatility-expansion pullback continuation. Candidate rule in the research note uses a completed-bar 5-minute realized-volatility ratio, a trend from completed hourly EMA20/EMA60, and a pullback/reclaim of 5-minute EMA20; enter at next bar open in trend direction, one position at a time, with a fixed maximum holding period and session flat. This is a conditional hypothesis with no direct NQ 5-minute strategy evidence in the supplied notes; short-lag return autocorrelation is an important counterargument. Its multiple intuitive parameters create high data-mining risk. Require stronger out-of-sample and fresh-data confirmation."}
{"id":"model_use_and_retrieval","topic":"modeling","content":"The supplied research notes explicitly advise against adding whole books or vague strategy prose to BUY/SELL/HOLD fine-tuning examples. Use sources to formulate explicit hypotheses, reproducible features/labels, and backtests. Benchmark the Qwen classifier against HOLD/no-trade, logistic regression, and gradient-boosted trees at matched trade coverage; the LLM must add stable out-of-sample net value to justify its complexity. A document retrieval system can provide relevant research passages to a separate language-model research assistant, but the current sequence-classification adapter does not automatically ingest or retrieve uploaded files. Retrieved text must not override frozen rules or directly authorize live orders."}
